  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 455 AM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nThe extended periods begin Wed night with a large high pressure \nridge centered just off the Carolina coast with its ridge axis \nextending from the TX/LA Gulf coast to the GA/SC Atlantic coast. \nThis ridge stays fairly stationary through Thu keeping things warm \nand dry but it does weaken slowly through the end of the week. \nTemperatures Thu and Fri will be in the 80s to near 90s with lows \nmainly in the 60s. As this ridge begins to weaken Thu, a fairly \norganized frontal boundary begins to move SE out of the Central \nPlains. It makes its way SE Thu night/Fri morning and into NW GA Fri \nafternoon. As this front moves SE both the ECMWF and the GFS show it \nloosing strength as a lot of its dynamics and moisture move NE \ncloser to the parent low center over the eastern Great Lakes and New \nEngland states. Just the southern tail end of this front swings SE \nthrough GA Fri night/Sat with the models showing it moving into N FL \njust after midnight Sun morning. If this holds true all we should \nsee from this frontal passage is maybe some increased cloud cover \nbut the biggest thing we will see is very Fall like temps moving \ninto the area for the weekend.\n\nThe models show a very strong high pressure ridge moving east out of \nthe Northern/Central Plains Sat and also building south into GA \nbehind the exiting frontal boundary. This ridge will bring a \ncooler/drier airmass to the region Sat with high temps expected in \nthe 60s and 70s. Low temps Sun morning will dip down into the 40s \nand 50s.  May even see some upper 30s across the NE GA mountains. \nSundays High temps expected to be in the 60s to lower 70s with some \n50s across the NE GA mountains. Looks like \"Sweater Weather\" is on \nthe horizon.",
